<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Arabia and the Gulf</p><h1 class="st-title">Ziyadid dynasty</h1><p class="st-meta">819 CE – 1018 CE (approx.) · capital Zabid</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The first Islamic dynasty of Yemen's coastal plain, which founded Zabid as its capital and ruled the Tihama for two centuries.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 818 or 819 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mamun">al-Ma'mun</a> sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-ziyad">Muhammad ibn Ziyad</a>, a descendant of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad</a> governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziyad-ibn-abihi">Ziyad ibn Abihi</a>, to subdue rebellious tribes in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tihama">Tihama</a>. He founded the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zabid">Zabid</a> as his base and established a hereditary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a>, recognising Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Ziyadids ruled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> coastal plain and at times claimed authority over <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-hadramawt">Hadramawt</a> and the highlands. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zabid">Zabid</a> became a walled city and a leading centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> learning. Real power often lay with African slave ministers; the Nubian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/husayn-ibn-salamah">Husayn ibn Salamah</a>, regent in the late 10th century, built mosques and roads linking Zabid with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> and Hadramawt.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In the early 11th century the last Ziyadid was a child under the control of slave commanders. The line ended around 1018, and after a struggle among these commanders Najah took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zabid">Zabid</a> around 1022, founding the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/najahid-dynasty">Najahid dynasty</a>. Zabid's mosques and scholarly tradition survived and made it a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage</a> city.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/ziyadid-dynasty.jpg" alt="Ziyadid dynasty" width="320" height="153"><figcaption>Image: H. Grobe · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jemen1988-022_hg.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>819 CE – 1018 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Zabid</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Muhammad ibn Ziyad, Husayn ibn Salamah</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Yemen</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Ziyadids, Banu Ziyad</dd></dl></aside></div>
